INT. OBSERVATORY DOME - SÉANCE - NIGHT

A great brass telescope tilts toward a slit of stars. A round oak table fills the center of the floor, ringed by four chairs. A single candle gutters. The dome creaks as it turns.

MADAME ZELDA VOSKUIJLEN (60s, turban, rings on every finger) presides, palms flat on the wood. To her left sits PROFESSOR EDMUND HARTWELL (70s, astronomer, pocket watch); to her right, a YOUNG WOMAN in mourning grey (20s, veil pushed back); opposite, a MAN in a damp overcoat who has given no name (40s, gloved).

MADAME ZELDA
Hands joined. Breathe. Is there a presence among us?

The candle gutters. Hartwell clears his throat.

HARTWELL
My dear, is this strictly necessary?

YOUNG WOMAN
Hush.

MADAME ZELDA
Is there a presence?

The telescope creaks as though a hand had nudged it. The dome stills. A single cold breath crosses the table.

MAN
(low)
Adelheid?

The Young Woman jerks her hand free.

YOUNG WOMAN
Who said that?

HARTWELL
(over her)
Edmund, I mean, I did not.

MADAME ZELDA
(over both)
Silence! Hands!

MAN
It's not the dead who answer. It's the clock in your pocket, Professor.

Hartwell pulls out the pocket watch. It is ticking backward.

HARTWELL
This is not possible.

The Young Woman stands, ripping off her veil. She is not young. She is ADELHEID HARTWELL (50s, his wife, long thought drowned), her face lit white by the candle.

ADELHEID
Hello, Edmund.

The Man removes his glove and lays a badge on the table. Hartwell sways.
